6. The B6 in hand. Maybe I can form a B2-B3-B4 Chow if I get B3 and B4, but I don't have them. Or maybe a T6-T7-T8 if I get T8. But without those, it's hard.\n\nIf I discard T4, then I have W3, W4, W9, W9, T2, T6, T7, B2, B6, B8. Still not helpful.\n\nIf I discard W3, then I lose the possibility of a W3-W4-W5 Chow, but maybe that's not useful.\n\nIf I discard B6, I have one less B6, but the meld already has one. Maybe others might need B6 for a Chow.\n\nIf I discard W9, I reduce the pair to one W9. If I can get another W9, that would form a pair, but I have two in hand. But there are three left in the wall. So maybe it's safe to discard one W9 now.\n\nAlternatively, if I keep both W9s as a pair, and try to form other melds. For example, if I can get a W5 to form W3-W4-W5, but that's not in my hand. Or maybe T3 for T2-T3-T4. But I don't have T3. So maybe not.\n\nAnother angle: looking at the unshown tiles. For example, W9 has 3 remaining. So if I discard a W9, there's a chance someone else might need it. But if I keep it, I have a pair. Maybe it's better to keep the pair for potential Pung or pair.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile that's not part of any possible meld. For example, B2. I don't have B3 or B4, so B2 is unlikely to form a Chow. Discarding B2 might be a good choice.\n\nSo between B2, B8, T4, etc. Let's see:\n\nDiscarding B2: it's a low tile, not part of any possible Chow in my hand. If I discard it, I remove a tile that's not helpful. That might be a good move.\n\nDiscarding B8: it's a middle tile, maybe useful for others. But if I don't need it, maybe it's okay.\n\nDiscarding T4: part of a possible T2-T4-T6 sequence, but not consecutive. So not helpful.\n\nDiscarding T2: part of a possible T2-T3-T4, but again, no T3.\n\nDiscarding T6: part of T6-T7-T8, need T8.\n\nDiscarding T7: part of T6-T7-T8.\n\nSo the best options are B2 or B8. Let's think about the unshown counts. B2 has 3 remaining, B8 has 3 remaining. So there are still some in the wall. If 