atively, maybe I can go for a Mixed Straight. That requires three Chows in different suits. But I have one in B, and maybe one in T. Need two more in different suits. \n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me think about the most points I can get. If I can form four Chows and a pair, that's All Chows (2 points). Plus other patterns. \n\nAlternatively, if I can form a Pung of W9, that's 2 points. If I have two Pungs, that's 4 points. \n\nLooking at my current hand, I have two W9s. If I can get another, that's a Pung. So maybe I should keep W9s. But I have to discard one tile. The options include playing W9, which would reduce my W9 count to one. But I have two W9s now. If I discard one, maybe I can get another later. \n\nBut the valid actions include Play W9. So if I play W9, that's an option. But why would I do that? Unless I think it's not useful. \n\nAlternatively, maybe playing T5 is better for forming another Chow. \n\nHmm. Let me try to outline possible hands after discarding each tile.\n\nIf I discard T5:\n\nHand: [W3, W4, W9, W9, T4, T6, T7, T8, B1, F2], Melds: [B5-B6-B7]\n\nPossible Chows: B5-B6-B7, T6-T7-T8. Then, I have T4 left. If I can get T5 or T3, but not. So maybe form a pair of T4 and another. Not helpful. \n\nIf I discard B1:\n\nHand: [W3, W4, W9, W9, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, F2], Melds: [B5-B6-B7]\n\nPossible Chows: B5-B6-B7, T4-T5-T6, T5-T6-T7, T6-T7-T8. But I have T4, T5, T6, T7, T8. If I can form two Chows here. For example, T4-T5-T6 and T7-T8-? But I don't have T9. So maybe T4-T5-T6 and T5-T6-T7. But that would require two T5s and two T6s. I have one of each. Not possible. \n\nIf I discard F2:\n\nHand: [W3, W4, W9, W9, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, B1], Melds: [B5-B6-B7]\n\nLose the Seat Wind tile. Maybe not ideal. \n\nIf I discard T4:\n\nHand: [W3, W4, W9, W9, T5, T6, T7, T8, B1, F2], Melds: [B5-B6-B7]\n\nPossible Chows: B5-B6-B7, T5-T6-T7, T6-T7-T8. That's two Chows. \n\nIf I discard W3:\n\nHand: [W4, W9, W9, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, B1, F2], Melds: [B5-B6-B7]\n\n